# Del 17 — keeps the `docker build` context small and, more importantly,
# keeps host-built artifacts out of the image entirely: a bin/obj folder
# built on Peter's Windows machine contains Windows-specific intermediate
# files that would either be dead weight or actively wrong inside the
# Linux build stage (src/Atlas.Api/Dockerfile restores and rebuilds
# everything from source anyway, so none of this is ever needed).

## .NET build output — rebuilt fresh inside the "build" stage every time
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/out/

## IDE / tooling
.vs/
.idea/
**/*.user

## Git and CI metadata — irrelevant inside the image
.git/
.github/
azure-pipelines.yml

## Test projects — never shipped in the runtime image; Atlas.Api.csproj
## doesn't reference them, but there's no reason to even copy them into the
## build context
tests/

## Documentation and generated docs — same reasoning as tests/
docs/
README.md

## Local runtime state some of the services in docker-compose.yml (or a
## plain `dotnet run`) generate on the host — never build inputs
.azurite/

# Note: Dockerfile/docker-compose.yml/.dockerignore themselves are
# deliberately NOT listed here. `docker build -f ...` always reads the
# Dockerfile straight off disk regardless of .dockerignore — this file only
# filters what COPY/ADD instructions can see — and src/Atlas.Api/Dockerfile's
# own `COPY src/ src/` step is narrow enough that excluding them would buy
# nothing anyway.
